<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.5.5: Using Graphical and Algebraic Approaches for Finding Unknowns of Linear Functions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00928</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.5.5: Using Graphical and Algebraic Approaches for Finding Unknowns of Linear Functions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>845fdf77-b1f3-49e5-b346-e262ae61cee1</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
<para id="para-00001">The college student who is looking for a data plan for her cell phone found a third option, which also offers a 2-gigabyte monthly allowance for data.</para>
<para id="para-00002">The monthly cost of this option can be represented by function \(C\), defined by \(C(x)=30x+5\), where \(x\) is the gigabytes of data used over the monthly allowance.</para>
<!--Question 2 -->
<exercise id="exer-00001">
  <problem id="prob-00001">
    <para id="para-00003">Find \(C(0.7)\) and explain what it means in this situation.</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00001">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00004">Compare your answers:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00005">\(C(0.7)=26\) because \(30(0.7)+5=26\). Your answers
      may vary, but here is a possible explanation: It means that the cost would be $26 if she used 0.7 gigabyte over the 2-gigabytes allowance in a month.
  </para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Question 2 -->
<exercise id="exer-00002">
  <problem id="prob-00002">
    <para id="para-00006">Her budget is still $50 a month. To find out how many gigabytes of data she could use if she chose this plan, she writes \(C(x)=50\) and solves for \(x\). What value of \(x\) makes this equation true? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00002">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00007">Compare your answers:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00008">\(x=1.5\). Your answers may vary, but here is a possible explanation: Because \(C(x)=30x+5\), we can write \(C(x)=50\) as \(30x+5=50\) and solve for \(x\), which gives 1.5.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise></section>
</content></document>